WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.940
In a previous video, we talked about the idea of Ludonarrative Dissonance:

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:10.660
A situation where the story being told by a game's mechanics disagrees with the story being told by a game's narrative,

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.880
and people had a lot of interesting and curious responses.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:20.600
But one example that people brought up stood out to me because it demonstrated, not a misunderstanding of dissonance,

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:25.180
but perhaps a misunderstanding of how games interact with narrative structure.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:31.300
So let's talk about the Stanley Parable, Dark Souls, and Intended Play.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.020
*Intro Music*

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:44.560
The Stanley Parable is a 2011 piece of First-person interactive fiction that revolves around a conflict between the player

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:50.500
protagonist, Stanley, and the game's narrator. As the player works Stanley through the game, the narrator

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.340
exposits his actions, including immediately upcoming actions that serve as

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:56.940
instructions.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:01.840
(Narrator): When Stanley came to a set of two of open doors, he entered the door on his left.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:04.140
The player chooses whether to follow these instructions

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.400
or to attempt to subvert them in some way.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.340
(Narrator): This was not the correct way to the meeting room, and Stanley knew it perfectly well.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:18.820
Dark souls, also published in 2011, is a third-person role-playing game where the player takes on the role of "The Chosen Undead," a

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.200
cursed human tasked by a vague

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:27.640
prophecy with rekindling the first flame to preserve the Age of Fire. Now, these two games are all in all very

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:33.520
different from one another but they're illustrative for our point here because they both utilize elements of

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:41.700
intentional disobedience. Games have a dimension of interaction that means there's not merely intended

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.780
interpretation but also

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:49.619
intended play. The intended interaction with a movie is pretty straightforward. The movie starts

00:01:49.619 --> 00:01:51.000
and you watch it.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:57.160
There are some subversive or alternative forms of watching, such as riffing a movie or the sing-along or

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:01.320
whatever the hell midnight screenings of The Room count as.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:06.840
*audience screaming the word spoons*

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:14.900
But generally speaking, it's pretty well bounded. This interaction in games means that games can do something that other media

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.800
rarely can outside a very experimental

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:25.220
context: which is to tell the participant to do one thing while expecting them to do another. The Stanley Parable

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:32.380
makes this process overt. The entire interaction paradigm of the game more or less revolves around this single principle,

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:35.160
which is what makes it so illustrative.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.040
But it's not actually a niche type of interaction. Any

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.640
open-world game has a moment where the player is told to do one thing and can then choose to

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.520
immediately turn around and go the other direction,

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.960
opting instead to spend dozens or even hundreds of hours doing

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:55.420
anything else but the thing that they were asked to do.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:03:02.880
Some games address this within the fiction, such as the Assassin's Creed games that suggests the player is playing a somewhat

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:08.700
scrambled version of the stories events. That, while Ezio may have jumped across all these roofs,

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:15.380
he didn't necessarily do it for five straight hours one afternoon instead of stabbing minor borges in the neck.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:19.620
Others, like Final Fantasy 7, opt to ignore the disconnect entirely,

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:25.760
operating on the logic that it's only weird if you call attention to it. Games are

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.280
constantly telling us to do one thing and then waiting to see if we

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:31.640
actually do it.

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:38.520
The Stanley Parable uses this to create a conflict between the player and the game, but at the end of the day that's just a

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.960
costume put on an otherwise ordinary

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:46.520
interaction. The Narrator is simply a disembodied antagonist, like SHODAN or GLaDOS, and,

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:53.080
much like GLaDOS, The Narrator of the Stanley Parable tells you to do or not do specific things

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:58.240
while the game fully expects you to ignore those requests.

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:00.160
(GLaDOS): Welcome to the final test.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:01.800
(GLaDOS): When you are done,

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:06.000
(GLaDOS): you will drop the device in the equipment recovery annex.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.000
Now those examples are all cases where a

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.819
character within the game issues explicit instructions,

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:19.200
and it's pretty clear that disobeying those instructions is core to the game, and possibly even the plot. Doing

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.600
what GLaDOS tells you to do

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.700
generally results in death.

00:04:23.709 --> 00:04:27.200
(GLaDOS): We are pleased that you made it through the final challenge,

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.920
(GLaDOS): where we pretended we were going to murder you.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:31.240
But these two

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:33.920
operating layers still exist even in less overt

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:40.120
games. Games are constantly telling us through various cues how the game expects us to behave in and

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:46.500
interact with its world. This is encapsulated in tutorials, but also environmental

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:53.140
communication and reinforcing desired play styles. The Assassin's Creed games are positively lousy with icons,

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:57.980
indicators, and things that blink and glow to draw your eye and encourage

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:01.480
interaction. It is a game series that sits on the verge of

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:08.760
screaming at you about the many things it wants you to do. As is the case with Dark Souls, the game may simply use standard

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:14.200
game language to imply that you shouldn't behave in a certain way, that some NPCs

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:22.120
you shouldn't kill because they're your friends, or maybe just your allies, or at the very least just because they're not

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:28.500
your enemies. These characters are friendly or they sell you things so you should leave them alone.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:32.920
That, the game says, is the correct way to behave.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:39.720
But that's simply a social contract between you and the game simulacrum of friends, and the game

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:42.320
absolutely intends for you to murder

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:47.580
every last one of them. If you go on a rampage in Dark Souls and kill

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:53.740
every last friendly NPC, you will actually unlock whole new side stories, and dungeons, and bosses.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:59.720
This is not simply emergent or procedural, this is intended play.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:04.639
If there is crafted content on the other side of your misbehavior,

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:13.220
then it's not actually misbehavior. Because it turns out that murdering everyone is also the correct way to behave.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:19.100
What a game intends for you to do includes not only the things the fiction of the game tells you to do

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:26.740
but also the broader things the game permits you to do. If there is something that the game's author simply flat-out,

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:31.680
100% did not want you to experience, did not want you to engage in,

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:38.780
then it wouldn't be there. Even when it comes to potential misbehavior using the rules of the system you're placed within,

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:44.260
externalities that they didn't or couldn't account for, like throwing garbage at NPCs,

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:49.380
the only truly accurate sign that the authors don't want you doing that is

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:57.220
that nothing happens at all. This is because even a negative reaction, even the game pushing back against misbehavior, is

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:05.320
content. This is a big challenge when writing about video games: that the text of a game includes not only the messages

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:11.600
intended and implied by the game's explicit plot and the ideas reinforced by the repetitive, mechanical

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:13.580
behaviors the game utilizes,

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:19.380
but also a variety of potentially dramatically diverging intended

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.700
postures of play. A reading of Dark Souls must encompass not just the nominal story

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:28.880
but also a breadth of possibilities and how those possibilities

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:34.440
alter the reading. This added complexity means that games as texts are harder to encompass.

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:38.980
But it also means that games as a medium provide a potentially far denser

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:41.540
package for meaning than other forms.

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:44.400
And I understand why this trips people up, this

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:48.800
idea that doing something the game is telling you not to do is

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:55.440
in fact what the game wants you to do because it's something the games authors have accounted for and have

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:00.940
programmed it to respond to. It's the treachery of images, but the treachery of

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:02.840
content.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:08.580
So what do you think? What's your favorite moment in a game

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:15.020
where it tells you not to do something that it ultimately intends for you to do, and why is it the "Genocide Route" in Undertale?

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:17.000
Let me know in the comments.

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:19.010
*Outro Music*

